ystem, when no valid witness supports it.","principles":[],"reader":{"analogy":{"boundary":"The picture only covers the misleading label; removing it says nothing about whether the prototype could ever be deployed.","maps":[{"analogy":"the 'production service' label on the prototype","doctrine":"the public page implying a power"},{"analogy":"the uptime, permissions and partnerships the prototype does not have","doctrine":"the set of unsupported powers"},{"analogy":"relabelling it 'local prototype' until a real deployment exists","doctrine":"cutting the claim back to its supported scope"}],"text":"Calling a local prototype a live production service. The name may help a reader see what the prototype resembles, but it gives the prototype none of the uptime, permissions or partnerships of a real production system."},"basis":"authored_reader_enrichment","counterexample":"A landing page reads as a ready, hosted product backed by an affiliated provider, when the system is a public reconstruction that hosts nothing, calls no provider and has no affiliation.","does_not_prove":"Blocking inflated wording says nothing about which powers the system does have.","enforced_in_component_ids":["batch12_release_claim_language_gate","public_reveal_walkthrough","cold_reader_route_map"],"example":{"component_ids":["batch12_release_claim_language_gate"],"text":"The release wording check scans the system's own public text for release-level language and blocks any line that implies hosting, readiness or a whole-system release the system does not have, so the line has to be reworded to its supported scope."},"governs":"What public pages say or imply about the system's powers.","plain":"The mistake of letting a public page's wording suggest powers the system does not have, such as being a hosted, ready product, having an outside partner, changing its own source, making financial decisions or being the same as the larger private system.","potential_misread":"That impressive wording is harmless because it costs nothing, as if stating a power were the same as having it.","refuses":"Public text implying a hosted service, outside service access, source changes, financial or trading decisions, production readiness, an affiliation or endorsement, or equivalence with the private system, when nothing supports it.","requires":"Stating only the powers that something checkable supports, and weakening or withdrawing a public claim when that support is missing.","source_ref":"core/doctrine_enrichment.json","why_it_matters":"Readers can rely on what a public page says about the system, such as being hosted, ready or affiliated, being limited to what the system can back."},"rests_on":[],"source_ref":"ANTI_PRINCIPLES.md::AP-14","statement":"| ID | Violated axioms | Rejected failure shape |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| AP-14 Public-authority inflation | AX-5, AX-8, AX-12 | Letting public docs imply a hosted service, access to an external service, changes to source files, financial or trading decisions, production readiness, an affiliation or endorsement, or equivalence with the private system, when no valid witness supports it. |","statement_sha256":"00be8c1208307c4a3b2b2d930c81835ac9dc0e213933b9fa8557a384dc68bc02","title":"Public-authority inflation","violates":["AX-5","AX-8","AX-12"]},"schema_version":"plectis_component_http_v1","snapshot_revision":"45be1deb333ad57cd9ae093229455d0a52d3bf23a9064cde09f1a6e16f4c7044"}